You can create a dynamic segment by wrapping a file or folder name in square brackets, such as `[slug]` [1, 3]. Routes generated this way allow you to create pages from data without manually creating a route for each individual item [3].

For example, you can create a dynamic route file at `app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx` and access the `params` prop to use the dynamic segment [3]:

```tsx filename="app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx" switcher
export default async function BlogPostPage({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>
}) {
  const { slug } = await params
  const post = await getPost(slug)

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      <p>{post.content}</p>
    </div>
  )
}
```

To automatically include a site name as a suffix for page titles, you can use `title.template` in a layout's metadata object [1]. 

When defining a `title.template`, a `default` title is required [1]. The `%s` placeholder in the template is replaced by the title defined in child route segments [1].

```tsx filename="app/layout.tsx"
import type { Metadata } from 'next'

export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: {
    template: '%s | Acme',
    default: 'Acme',
  },
}
```[1]

To show a fallback UI when an unexpected runtime error occurs, you can use Next.js error boundaries. 

For route segments in the App Router, create an `error.js` file inside the route segment to automatically wrap it in an error boundary [1, 3]. 

For component-level error recovery anywhere in your component tree, you can use the `catchError` function to create a custom error boundary wrapper [3, 4, 5]:

```tsx filename="app/custom-error-boundary.tsx"
'use client'

import { catchError, type ErrorInfo } from 'next/error'

function ErrorFallback(props: { title: string }, { error, retry }: ErrorInfo) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{props.title}</h2>
      <button onClick={() => retry()}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )
}

export default catchError(ErrorFallback)
```

To customize the 404 page in the App Router, you can use `not-found.js` for route-level missing resources or `global-not-found.js` for URLs that do not match any route at all [1, 4, 5]. 

You can trigger the route-level not-found UI by calling the `notFound()` function inside a component, Server Function, or Route Handler when a resource is missing [3, 5].

```tsx filename="app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx"
import { notFound } from 'next/navigation'

export default async function Page({ params }) {
  const { slug } = await params
  const post = getPostBySlug(slug)

  if (!post) {
    notFound()
  }

  return <div>{post.title}</div>
}
```[5]

For an application-wide 404 page when a URL doesn't match any route, enable the `globalNotFound` flag in `next.config.ts` and create an `app/global-not-found.tsx` file that returns a full HTML document containing `<html>` and `<body>` tags [4]. Both the root `not-found.js` and `global-not-found.js` support exporting a `metadata` object or `generateMetadata` function to customize head tags [1].

You can build a JSON API endpoint inside the `app` directory by creating a Route Handler (e.g., `app/api/route.ts`) and returning a response with a JSON body using `NextResponse.json()` or `Response.json()` [1, 3]. 

```ts filename="app/api/route.ts"
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server'

export async function GET(request: Request) {
  return NextResponse.json({ error: 'Internal Server Error' }, { status: 500 })
}
```

To make a component run in the browser and use features like `useState` and `onClick`, you need to create a Client Component by adding the `'use client'` directive at the very top of the file, before any imports [1, 5]. 

```tsx filename="app/components/counter.tsx"
'use client'

import { useState } from 'react'

export default function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

Once a file is marked with `'use client'`, it establishes a boundary, and all of its imports and directly rendered components will be included in the client bundle [5]. Remember that any props passed to this component from the server must be serializable [1].

To allow the Image component to load pictures hosted on an external domain, you must configure the `remotePatterns` property in your `next.config.js` file [4, 5]. 

```js filename="next.config.js"
module.exports = {
  images: {
    remotePatterns: [
      {
        protocol: 'https',
        hostname: 'example.com',
      },
    ],
  },
}
```

The `images.domains` configuration can also be used, but it has been deprecated since Next.js 14 in favor of the stricter `remotePatterns` for better security [1, 2, 3].

You can use Google Fonts without sending requests to Google from the browser by using the `next/font/google` module [1, 3]. 

Next.js includes built-in automatic self-hosting for any font file [1, 3]. When you use `next/font`, the CSS and font files are downloaded at build time and self-hosted alongside your other static assets, completely removing external network requests for improved privacy and performance [1, 3].

```tsx filename="app/layout.tsx"
import { Inter } from 'next/font/google'

const inter = Inter({
  subsets: ['latin'],
})

export default function RootLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode
}) {
  return (
    <html lang="en" className={inter.className}>
      <body>{children}</body>
    </html>
  )
}
```[3]

You can read an incoming request cookie in a Server Component using the async `cookies` function from `next/headers` [4]. 

```tsx filename="app/page.tsx" switcher
import { cookies } from 'next/headers'

export default async function Page() {
  const cookieStore = await cookies()
  const theme = cookieStore.get('theme')
  return '...'
}
```

To prerender all blog post pages at build time, supply the full list of paths by returning them from the `generateStaticParams` function in your dynamic segment page [2]:

```tsx filename="app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx"
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  const posts = await fetch('https://.../posts').then((res) => res.json())

  return posts.map((post) => ({
    slug: post.slug,
  }))
}

export default async function Page({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>
}) {
  const { slug } = await params
  // ...
}
```

You must always return an array from `generateStaticParams`, even if it is empty [2]. When using ⁠`fetch` inside `generateStaticParams`, requests are automatically deduplicated to speed up build time [3].

You can render multiple independent sections like team and analytics at the same time by using **parallel routes** [1, 5]. Parallel routes are created using named slots with the `@folder` convention (such as `@team` and `@analytics`) [1]. 

These slots are passed as props to the shared parent layout component, allowing you to render them alongside the `children` prop in parallel [1]:

```tsx filename="app/layout.tsx"
export default function Layout({
  children,
  team,
  analytics,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode
  analytics: React.ReactNode
  team: React.ReactNode
}) {
  return (
    <>
      {children}
      {team}
      {analytics}
    </>
  )
}
```

To forward requests to another backend without changing the URL the user sees, you can use `rewrites` in `next.config.js` or a proxy rewrite [3, 4]. Rewrites map an incoming request path to a different destination path while keeping the URL unchanged in the browser [3]. 

For example, you can configure rewrites in `next.config.js`:

```js filename="next.config.js"
module.exports = {
  async rewrites() {
    return [
      {
        source: '/api/:path*',
        destination: 'https://api.example.com/api/:path*',
      },
    ]
  },
}
```

To navigate to another page from an event handler like a button click in a Client Component, you can use the `push` method from the `useRouter` hook imported from `next/navigation` [4]. 

```tsx filename="app/page.tsx"
'use client'

import { useRouter } from 'next/navigation'

export default function Page() {
  const router = useRouter()

  return (
    <button type="button" onClick={() => router.push('/dashboard')}>
      Dashboard
    </button>
  )
}
```[4]

You can handle form submissions directly on the server by using Server Actions [4]. Server Actions always execute on the server and can be invoked directly by passing them to the form's `action` attribute [3, 4].

```tsx filename="app/ui/signup-form.tsx"
import { signup } from '@/app/actions/auth'

export function SignupForm() {
  return (
    <form action={signup}>
      <input name="email" type="email" placeholder="Email" />
      <button type="submit">Sign Up</button>
    </form>
  )
}
```[4]

Editors can preview unpublished content from a headless CMS by using Next.js features like **Draft Mode** (or the deprecated **Preview Mode**) [2, 4, 5]. These features allow Next.js to temporarily bypass static generation and render pages at request time instead of build time [3, 4].

To fetch draft data, you can update `getStaticProps` to check whether `context.draftMode` (or `context.preview`) is true, and then modify your API endpoint to fetch draft content rather than published content [1, 2]. 

```js
export async function getStaticProps(context) {
  const url = context.draftMode
    ? 'https://draft.example.com'
    : 'https://production.example.com'
  const res = await fetch(url)
  // ...
}
```

To catch typos in link `href` properties using TypeScript, you need to enable the `typedRoutes` option in your `next.config.js` or `next.config.ts` file [1, 2]. This feature works in projects using TypeScript for both the Pages and App Router's `next/link` component, as well as `next/navigation` methods like `push`, `replace`, and `prefetch` in the App Router [2].

```js filename="next.config.js"
/** @type {import('next').NextConfig} */
const nextConfig = {
  typedRoutes: true,
}

module.exports = nextConfig
```

Next.js automatically generates a link definition file inside `.next/types` containing all existing routes, which allows your editor and the TypeScript compiler to validate literal `href` strings and warn you about invalid paths [2]. For non-literal strings, you may need to manually cast the path using `as Route` [2].

To fetch fresh data on every request in the Pages Router, use Server-side Rendering (also known as dynamic rendering) by exporting an async function named `getServerSideProps` from your page [1, 5]. 

This function runs on the server on every request [1, 3], allowing you to fetch data directly from a database, CMS, or external API [3, 5], and pass it to the page component via props [1, 3]:

```jsx
export default function Page({ data }) {
  return <p>{data}</p>
}

export async function getServerSideProps() {
  const res = await fetch(`https://.../data`)
  const data = await res.json()

  return { props: { data } }
}
```[1]

To rebuild a static page in the background at most once per minute, you can use the `revalidate` property set to `60` (seconds) inside your `getStaticProps` function [3]. 

```js
export async function getStaticProps() {
  const res = await fetch('https://.../posts')
  const posts = await res.json()

  return {
    props: {
      posts,
    },
    revalidate: 60, // In seconds (once per minute)
  }
}
```

You can run background work like logging or analytics after a response is sent using either `after` or `waitUntil` [1, 2]. 

`after` schedules work to be executed after a response or prerender finishes, and can be used in Server Components, Server Functions, Route Handlers, and Proxy [2].

```tsx filename="app/layout.tsx"
import { after } from 'next/server'
import { log } from '@/app/utils'

export default function Layout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  after(() => {
    log()
  })
  return <>{children}</>
}
```[2]

Alternatively, in Proxy, you can use the `waitUntil()` method provided by `NextFetchEvent` to keep the invocation alive until your background promise settles [1, 3].

To load a third-party script only when the browser is idle, you can use the `lazyOnload` strategy with the `next/script` component [1, 3]. This strategy injects the script client-side during browser idle time after all resources have been fetched [3].

```jsx filename="app/page.js"
import Script from 'next/script'

export default function Page() {
  return (
    <>
      <Script src="https://example.com/script.js" strategy="lazyOnload" />
    </>
  )
}
```

You can set the title and description of a page in the App Router by exporting a static `metadata` object from a `layout.js` or `page.js` file [1, 4]. 

```tsx filename="app/blog/layout.tsx"
import type { Metadata } from 'next'

export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: 'My Blog',
  description: '...',
}

export default function Layout() {}
```

Alternatively, you can define metadata using the `generateMetadata` function [2, 3].

Yes, many webpack loaders work with Turbopack, including SVG loaders like `@svgr/webpack` and `svg-inline-loader` [1, 2]. You can configure them in `next.config.js` under the `turbopack.rules` configuration [1]. 

Here is an example of configuring an SVG loader:

```js filename="next.config.js"
module.exports = {
  turbopack: {
    rules: {
      '*.svg': {
        loaders: ['@svgr/webpack'],
        as: '*.js',
      },
    },
  },
}
```

Keep in mind that Turbopack only supports a core subset of the webpack loader API, and loaders must return JavaScript code (loaders that transform files like stylesheets or images directly are not supported) [1].
